  "query_preview": "..A strong warming trend will occur through Monday as \na ridge builds into the region and amplifies across the western \nUnited States. High temperatures will warm to 10-15 degrees above \nnormal Sunday and Monday. Isolated areas west of Tucson will be in \nthe Major category for HeatRisk on Monday, which is expected to be \nthe hottest day. Highs Monday will be 101-103 degrees in the Tucson \nmetro and 105-108 degrees in the far western deserts near Ajo and \nOrgan Pipe. \n\nBreezy northwest winds will occur through the early evening hours \ntonight in the upper Gila River valley around Safford, with wind \nspeeds of 15-20 mph and gusts to around 30 mph. Lighter winds are \nexpected Sunday, and then gusty east winds on Monday as surface high \npressure builds down the front range of the Rockies into west Texas. \nA few locations may see easterly gusts to 30-35 mph Monday morning, \nbefore tapering off Monday afternoon. \n\nHigh temperatures lower several degrees Tuesday through Thursday of \nnext week as the ridge axis slides off to our east, but will still \nremain 4-7 degrees above normal. As the ridge axis shifts to our \neast, the steering flow turns south/southeasterly which may advect \nsome mid-level moisture into our eastern areas (mainly east of \nTucson). Measurable rainfall chances at this time are very low. \nHowever, there may be enough moisture for dry thunderstorms and/or \ngusty outflow winds. Thunder probabilities in the Tuesday through \nThursday time frame of next week are in the 5-15 percent range from \nTucson eastward. By late next week, a shortwave trough passes to our \nnorth, pushing the mid level moisture off to our east.",
